Compile-probe patch for avito-tech/go-mutesting v2.3.1 (MUT-6-27).

Upstream reads three meanings out of one `go test` exit code: 0 for tests
passing, 1 for tests failing, and 2 for a build failure it maps to SKIP. Modern
`go test` returns 1 for a package build failure, so the skip path is unreachable
and every mutant that never compiled is counted as a kill. Measured on this
repository, that was 11.07% of dp mutants, 16.99% of human, and roughly 9.07% of
techniques.

The patch compiles each mutant with `go test -c` before running the suite and
returns the skip verdict on failure, so compilation is observed rather than
inferred from an exit code. `go test -c` is the faithful discriminator: `go
build` returns 0 when only a test file fails to compile, and `go vet` runs a
wider analyser set than `go test` does.

Applied by `make mutation-install`, which fails the build if this patch stops
applying. Refresh it against the new tag rather than building without it.

diff --git a/cmd/go-mutesting/main.go b/cmd/go-mutesting/main.go
index 5749905..dd84faa 100644
--- a/cmd/go-mutesting/main.go
+++ b/cmd/go-mutesting/main.go
@@ -48,6 +48,14 @@ const (
 	returnError
 )
 
+// exitCodeDidNotCompile is the exec verdict the caller maps to SkippedCount.
+const exitCodeDidNotCompile = 2
+
+// compileProbeMarker identifies this local patch inside the built binary, so an
+// unpatched or stale go-mutesting can be detected before it is trusted to
+// produce a score. Keep it in sync with GO_MUTESTING_PATCH_MARKER in api/Makefile.
+const compileProbeMarker = "go-mutesting-compile-probe/MUT-6-27"
+
 func checkArguments(args []string, opts *models.Options) (bool, int) {
 	p := flags.NewNamedParser("go-mutesting", flags.None)
 
@@ -464,6 +472,33 @@ func mutateExec(
 			pkgName += "/..."
 		}
 
+		// A mutant that does not compile is not a kill. `go test` exits 1 for a
+		// build failure exactly as it does for a failing test, so the exit-code
+		// switch below cannot tell the two apart and scores every non-compiling
+		// mutant as killed. Observe compilation directly instead of inferring it
+		// from an exit code, which is the assumption that produced the defect.
+		// `go test -c` is the faithful discriminator: it compiles the test files
+		// and runs the same default vet subset `go test` does, where `go build`
+		// does neither and `go vet` runs a wider analyser set.
+		if opts.Test.Recursive {
+			panic(compileProbeMarker + ": --test-recursive is not supported, because `go test -c` rejects a multi-package pattern; run one package at a time")
+		}
+		compileCmd := exec.Command("go", "test", "-c", "-o", os.DevNull, pkgName)
+		compileCmd.Env = os.Environ()
+		if compileOut, compileErr := compileCmd.CombinedOutput(); compileErr != nil {
+			if opts.General.Verbose {
+				fmt.Println("Mutation did not compile (" + compileProbeMarker + ")")
+			}
+			if opts.General.Debug {
+				fmt.Printf("%s\n", compileOut)
+				console.PrintDiff(diff)
+			}
+
+			mutant.Diff = string(diff)
+
+			return exitCodeDidNotCompile
+		}
+
 		goTestCmd := exec.Command("go", "test", "-timeout", fmt.Sprintf("%ds", opts.Exec.Timeout), pkgName)
 		goTestCmd.Env = os.Environ()
 
